"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,928\nThe Learning Brain\n\n2\n00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:06,539\nThe cerebellum: timing and practice\n\n3\n00:00:09,539 --> 00:00:11,296\nPut on goggles fitted with prisms,\n\n4\n00:00:11,316 --> 00:00:13,676\nand the world appears shifted to one side.\n\n5\n00:00:14,188 --> 00:00:16,175\nIn a 1996 study,\n\n6\n00:00:16,323 --> 00:00:19,626\npeople threw balls of clay at a target while wearing them.\n\n7\n00:00:20,057 --> 00:00:22,896\nAt first, every throw missed to the side.\n\n8\n00:00:23,378 --> 00:00:25,334\nThrow by throw, they corrected.\n\n9\n00:00:25,725 --> 00:00:26,954\nWhen the goggles came off,\n\n10\n00:00:26,974 --> 00:00:28,570\nthey missed to the other side.\n\n11\n00:00:29,027 --> 00:00:32,494\nPeople with damage to the cerebellum adapted much less.\n\n12\n00:00:34,664 --> 00:00:37,389\nThe cerebellum, Latin for little brain,\n\n13\n00:00:37,593 --> 00:00:39,035\nsits at the back of the head,\n\n14\n00:00:39,230 --> 00:00:40,762\nbelow the rest of the brain.\n\n15\n00:00:41,274 --> 00:00:46,462\nIt holds about 69 billion of the brain's 86 billion neurons.\n\n16\n00:00:48,632 --> 00:00:50,407\nIt works like an error corrector.\n\n17\n00:00:50,786 --> 00:00:54,249\nIt compares the movement you meant to make with what actually happened,\n\n18\n00:00:54,434 --> 00:00:55,873\nand adjusts the next one.\n\n19\n00:00:56,349 --> 00:00:59,090\nSmall errors, corrected again and again,\n\n20\n00:00:59,215 --> 00:01:00,323\ntune a skill.\n\n21\n00:01:02,493 --> 00:01:04,110\nIt also learns timing.\n\n22\n00:01:04,515 --> 00:01:07,673\nWhen a tone is followed by a puff of air to the eye,\n\n23\n00:01:07,991 --> 00:01:11,712\nanimals and people learn to blink just before the puff arrives.\n\n24\n00:01:12,114 --> 00:01:14,397\nWhen the cerebellum is damaged,\n\n25\n00:01:14,504 --> 00:01:16,584\nthat well-timed blink is lost.\n\n26\n00:01:18,754 --> 00:01:20,528\nWhy does this matter for learning?\n\n27\n00:01:20,780 --> 00:01:23,615\nSkills improve through feedback on errors.\n\n28\n00:01:23,961 --> 00:01:26,085\nPractise with quick, specific feedback,\n\n29\n00:01:26,105 --> 00:01:27,738\nso you can see each miss.\n\n30\n00:01:27,992 --> 00:01:29,878\nExpect early mistakes.\n\n31\n00:01:30,195 --> 00:01:32,848\nThey are the signal the cerebellum learns from.\n\n32\n00:01:33,018 --> 00:01:35,447\nThe cerebellum turns errors into skill.\n\n33\n00:01:35,805 --> 00:01:37,770\nEvery miss teaches the next move.\n\n"
